<!--
  Mints an access token for the client credentials grant. The verification half
  shipped first and no way to get a token came with it, so a caller had to
  already hold one from somewhere this repository does not build.

  Four elements here are present because their absence is the behaviour
  nobody chose.

  SupportedGrantTypes is Presence Required and the page still names a default.
  "If no supported grant types are specified, then the only allowed grant
  types are authorization_code and implicit." So a GenerateAccessToken policy
  written without it mints tokens for two interactive grants and refuses the
  one a service to service caller uses. The element is the whole difference
  between a token endpoint for machines and a token endpoint for browsers.

  ExpiresIn is in milliseconds and the response field is in seconds. The page
  says so in two sentences a paragraph apart. It also says that without the
  element "the system applies a default value configured at the system level"
  and gives no number for it, so a token endpoint shipped without ExpiresIn
  has a lifetime nobody here can state. 3600000 is one hour. A value of -1
  takes the platform maximum and reports expires_in as 0, and any other
  negative or zero is a deployment error rather than a runtime one.

  GenerateResponse defaults to false, which populates flow variables and
  returns nothing to the caller. A token endpoint that mints a token and does
  not hand it over is the shape of this defect that is hardest to see, because
  every policy succeeded.

  GenerateErrorResponse defaults to false too. With it off, a refusal reaches
  the caller as whatever the proxy does with an unhandled fault.

  TODO: where the client secret is read from is not documented on the
  OAuthV2 page. ClientId has an element, a default of
  request.formparam.client_id, and a sentence saying any other variable is
  unsupported. There is no matching element for the secret and no sentence
  naming its source. The only mention on the page sits in the invalid_client
  fault row, which says these values are typically sent as a Base64 encoded
  Basic Authorization header. Typically is not a specification, so this bundle
  does not add a BasicAuthentication policy on the strength of it. See
  apigee/semantics.py UNANSWERED.
-->
<OAuthV2 async="false" continueOnError="false" enabled="true" name="OA-GenerateToken">
    <DisplayName>Mint an access token</DisplayName>
    <Operation>GenerateAccessToken</Operation>
    <SupportedGrantTypes>
        <GrantType>client_credentials</GrantType>
    </SupportedGrantTypes>
    <!-- Named although it is the default, because the element is doing work.
         It says the grant type arrives as a form parameter in the request
         body rather than as a query parameter. -->
    <GrantType>request.formparam.grant_type</GrantType>
    <!-- The only supported value. The page says setting ClientId to any
         other variable is not supported. -->
    <ClientId>request.formparam.client_id</ClientId>
    <ExpiresIn>3600000</ExpiresIn>
    <GenerateResponse enabled="true"/>
    <GenerateErrorResponse enabled="true"/>
</OAuthV2>
